      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "(c) 2007 Wiley-Liss, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": "We report the case of an 85-year-old man who presented with worsening exertional dyspnoea 18 months after coronary artery bypass surgery for critical three vessel coronary artery disease. Coronary angiography revealed an anastomotic stricture between a radial artery graft and the first diagonal branch. Percutaneous coronary intervention utilizing a retrograde application of the \"buddy\" wire technique was then performed."
      },
